{#
    The other footer: a site's, rather than a surface's.

    The columns are the site itself — the top level of the toctree, a column
    per section, and under each of them the pages it holds. Written out by hand
    they were a second copy of the tree that no check could see going stale: a
    page added under a section reaches the rail and the breadcrumb by itself,
    and the footer kept whatever somebody typed the day it was written.

    Two levels, and no deeper. A section and its pages is a footer; a section,
    its pages and their sections is the sitemap, and a reader who wants that
    wants a page, not the end of every page.

    A page that stands directly under the root is not a section and does not
    get a column to itself — it joins the site's own, the one carrying the
    product's name. A column whose heading and only entry say the same word
    twice is a column saying it once, and three of them in a row read as a
    fault in the renderer.

    `soul.footer.groups` is what the tree cannot know — a licence, a product
    site, somewhere else entirely — and those columns follow the site's own.
    Everything else here is configuration too; see `SoulExtension`. With
    nothing configured and no tree this is the mark and the note, which is the
    least a page can say and still be honest about what it is.
#}
{#- The columns and the line under them, as data. `sds-footer` takes groups, a
    note and the marks — that is its form — and this file's job is to work out
    what the tree says and hand it over. It used to build the footer itself,
    `sds-footer__group` and `sds-footer__links` and all, which is the element's
    own markup written in a file that is not the element: a column that grew a
    rule, or a heading that changed register, would have had to be changed in
    both, and only one of them is checked.

    A link inside the project is a *document*, not a path: `/frontend` resolves
    per page, while `frontend.html` resolves from wherever the reader happens
    to be standing and lands in nothing one directory down. A menu entry that
    carries a URL is somebody else's site and is left alone.

    `#` is what the renderer answers for the page being rendered — its way of
    saying "you are here" — and the element draws that item as the current one
    rather than as a link that goes nowhere. -#}
{%- set configured = soul.footer.groups is defined ? soul.footer.groups : [] -%}
{%- set entries = [] -%}
{%- for menu in env.projectNode.globalMenues -%}
    {%- set entries = entries|merge(menu.menuEntries) -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- set pages = entries|filter(entry => entry.children|default([]) is empty) -%}
{%- set sections = entries|filter(entry => entry.children|default([]) is not empty) -%}
{%- set product = soul.product|default(env.projectNode.title)|default('') -%}

{%- set groups = [] -%}
{#- A page that stands directly under the root joins the site's own column, the
    one carrying the product's name. -#}
{%- if pages -%}
    {%- set items = [] -%}
    {%- for page in pages -%}
        {%- set away = '://' in page.url -%}
        {%- set items = items|merge([{ label: page.value.toString, href: away ? page.url : renderLink(page.url), external: away }]) -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
    {%- set groups = groups|merge([{ label: product, items: items }]) -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- for section in sections -%}
    {%- set items = [] -%}
    {%- for page in section.children -%}
        {%- set away = '://' in page.url -%}
        {%- set items = items|merge([{ label: page.value.toString, href: away ? page.url : renderLink(page.url), external: away }]) -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
    {#- The heading is the section's own page. A column names a section the
        reader can otherwise only reach from the bar, and the bar is a written
        list of front doors that this one may not be on — which is how a
        section with pages under it ended up published and linked from
        nowhere. -#}
    {%- set groups = groups|merge([{ label: section.value.toString, href: renderLink(section.url), items: items }]) -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{#- What the tree cannot know — a licence, a product site, somewhere else
    entirely — following the site's own columns. -#}
{%- for group in configured -%}
    {%- set items = [] -%}
    {%- for entry in group.links -%}
        {%- set away = entry.external|default(false) -%}
        {%- set items = items|merge([{ label: entry.label, href: away ? entry.href : renderLink(entry.href), external: away }]) -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
    {%- set groups = groups|merge([{ label: group.title|default(''), items: items }]) -%}
{%- endfor -%}

{#- The mark of the service, which is what `sds-footer` draws at the end of
    its line: the glyph alone, named for whoever cannot see it. Which mark it
    is comes from the URL — see `Brands` — so nothing here can name a service
    the link does not go to, and a host with no mark in the set stays the
    labelled link it already was. -#}
{%- set marks = [] -%}
{%- for social in soul.footer.socials|default([]) -%}
    {%- set mark = { label: social.label, href: social.href, external: true } -%}
    {%- if social.icon %}{% set mark = mark|merge({ icon: social.icon }) %}{% endif -%}
    {%- set marks = marks|merge([mark]) -%}
{%- endfor -%}

{#- The mark, at the other end of the site from the bar and drawn out of the
    same setting. A footer that names the site it ends is the one place a
    reader who scrolled to the bottom can be told where they are; the bar has
    gone by then. -#}
<sds-footer
    groups="{{ groups|json_encode }}"
    product="{{ product }}"
    {%- if soul.signet %} signet="{{ asset('/' ~ soul.signet) }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if soul.brand %} brand="{{ soul.brand }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if soul.footer.note is defined and soul.footer.note %} note="{{ soul.footer.note }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if env.projectNode.copyright %} copyright="{{ env.projectNode.copyright }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if marks %} marks="{{ marks|json_encode }}"{% endif -%}
></sds-footer>
